WEBVTT

0
00:00.210 --> 00:02.140
<v Krista>In this video, we're talking about divisibility</v>

1
00:02.140 --> 00:04.310
and some of the tricks that we use to say

2
00:04.310 --> 00:06.630
whether or not a number is divisible by two, three,

3
00:06.630 --> 00:09.680
four, five, six, seven, eight, nine, or 10.

4
00:09.680 --> 00:11.670
There are a lot more divisibility rules

5
00:11.670 --> 00:12.970
than we're going to cover here.

6
00:12.970 --> 00:15.180
But these are the ones that you're going to use most commonly

7
00:15.180 --> 00:16.220
because you're going to be looking to see

8
00:16.220 --> 00:17.900
whether or not a number is divisible

9
00:17.900 --> 00:20.060
by some of these lower numbers here.

10
00:20.060 --> 00:21.930
So we're going to talk about the divisibility rules

11
00:21.930 --> 00:23.150
first for two.

12
00:23.150 --> 00:24.480
This one's going to be the most obvious

13
00:24.480 --> 00:26.450
out of all of the divisibility rules.

14
00:26.450 --> 00:28.810
It's just if the number is an even number,

15
00:28.810 --> 00:30.840
then it's going to be divisible by two.

16
00:30.840 --> 00:33.040
The way that you know for sure if it's even

17
00:33.040 --> 00:37.200
is if the last digit is zero, two, four, six, or eight.

18
00:37.200 --> 00:39.080
So if the last digit is even,

19
00:39.080 --> 00:41.150
then the number is divisible by two.

20
00:41.150 --> 00:45.560
So because all of these numbers here, 120, 126, and 128

21
00:45.560 --> 00:46.990
have an even last digit,

22
00:46.990 --> 00:49.750
zero is an even number, six is an even number,

23
00:49.750 --> 00:51.260
eight is an even number,

24
00:51.260 --> 00:54.800
and is included in the set zero, two, four, six, or eight,

25
00:54.800 --> 00:56.290
we know that all three of these numbers

26
00:56.290 --> 00:57.410
are divisible by two.

27
00:57.410 --> 01:01.150
So we can go ahead and say these are all divisible by two.

28
01:01.150 --> 01:03.410
Now what about divisibility by three?

29
01:03.410 --> 01:05.070
Well in that case, the trick that we're going to use

30
01:05.070 --> 01:08.420
is we're going to add up all of the digits in our number

31
01:08.420 --> 01:10.790
and see if the sum is divisible by three.

32
01:10.790 --> 01:12.930
So in this first number here, 120,

33
01:12.930 --> 01:14.190
we're going to add up the digits.

34
01:14.190 --> 01:15.970
The digits are one, two, and zero,

35
01:15.970 --> 01:17.350
so we're going to add those together.

36
01:17.350 --> 01:19.840
One plus two plus zero is three.

37
01:19.840 --> 01:21.630
And three is divisible by three,

38
01:21.630 --> 01:24.130
so 120 is divisible by three.

39
01:24.130 --> 01:26.470
If we add up the digits in 126,

40
01:26.470 --> 01:27.980
one plus two is three.

41
01:27.980 --> 01:29.640
Three plus six is nine.

42
01:29.640 --> 01:31.380
Nine is divisible by three.

43
01:31.380 --> 01:34.410
So 126 is also divisible by three.

44
01:34.410 --> 01:37.780
If we look at 128, we get one plus two is three.

45
01:37.780 --> 01:39.560
Three plus eight is 11.

46
01:39.560 --> 01:42.280
11 is not evenly divisible by three.

47
01:42.280 --> 01:44.200
We would get 3 2/3.

48
01:44.200 --> 01:46.570
Three doesn't go evenly into 11.

49
01:46.570 --> 01:51.050
So that means that 128 is not divisible by three.

50
01:51.050 --> 01:53.800
What about the divisibility rule for four?

51
01:53.800 --> 01:55.630
What we're going to do is look at the last two digits

52
01:55.630 --> 01:58.800
of the number and see if that number is divisible by four.

53
01:58.800 --> 02:01.600
So in this example here, we have the number 120.

54
02:01.600 --> 02:03.720
We're just going to look at the last two digits.

55
02:03.720 --> 02:06.520
In that case, the last two digits is the number 20.

56
02:06.520 --> 02:08.670
Since 20 is divisible by four,

57
02:08.670 --> 02:10.650
four goes into 20 five times,

58
02:10.650 --> 02:14.070
that means that 120 is also divisible by four.

59
02:14.070 --> 02:16.120
So that one's going to be divisible by four.

60
02:16.120 --> 02:19.160
If we look at 126, we're only looking at the last two digits

61
02:19.160 --> 02:20.720
so we look at 26.

62
02:20.720 --> 02:23.062
Is 26 evenly divisible by four?

63
02:23.062 --> 02:26.160
No, four doesn't go evenly into 26.

64
02:26.160 --> 02:30.510
So 126 is not going to be divisible by four.

65
02:30.510 --> 02:34.970
If we look at 128, we only look at the last two digits, 28,

66
02:34.970 --> 02:36.810
and four goes evenly into 28.

67
02:36.810 --> 02:38.260
It goes in seven times.

68
02:38.260 --> 02:41.320
So 128 will be divisible by four.

69
02:41.320 --> 02:43.760
Five is an easy one, and should make sense to us.

70
02:43.760 --> 02:47.580
We're just looking for numbers that end in zero or in five.

71
02:47.580 --> 02:49.910
So 120 is divisible by five

72
02:49.910 --> 02:52.220
because the last digit is a zero.

73
02:52.220 --> 02:55.389
But 126 and 128 are not divisible by five

74
02:55.389 --> 02:58.620
because the last digit is not a five or a zero.

75
02:58.620 --> 03:01.320
Six and eight are not five or zero.

76
03:01.320 --> 03:04.240
So these two numbers are not divisible by five.

77
03:04.240 --> 03:06.710
Since the divisibility rules for two and three

78
03:06.710 --> 03:09.720
are pretty quick, we like to use those as tests

79
03:09.720 --> 03:12.440
to figure out whether or not the number is divisible by six.

80
03:12.440 --> 03:15.560
If the number is divisible by both two and three,

81
03:15.560 --> 03:18.380
then it's also divisible by six.

82
03:18.380 --> 03:20.970
Since 120 is divisible by two and three,

83
03:20.970 --> 03:22.900
we know it's also divisible by six.

84
03:22.900 --> 03:26.270
And since 126 is divisible by both two and three,

85
03:26.270 --> 03:28.260
we know it's also divisible by six.

86
03:28.260 --> 03:32.440
However, since 128 is divisible by two but not by three,

87
03:32.440 --> 03:35.660
it's not going to be divisible by six.

88
03:35.660 --> 03:38.520
The rule for seven is a little bit harder to remember,

89
03:38.520 --> 03:39.353
but here's what it is.

90
03:39.353 --> 03:41.110
We take the last digit in the number,

91
03:41.110 --> 03:43.470
so for 120 we take zero.

92
03:43.470 --> 03:47.080
We multiply it by five, so zero times five is zero.

93
03:47.080 --> 03:50.060
And then we add that result to the rest of the number.

94
03:50.060 --> 03:52.430
So here the rest of the number is 12.

95
03:52.430 --> 03:55.710
So we take 12 plus zero and we get 12.

96
03:55.710 --> 03:58.750
Is 12, that result, divisible by seven?

97
03:58.750 --> 04:02.120
No, it's not, which means that 120 is not going to be

98
04:02.120 --> 04:04.130
divisible by seven.

99
04:04.130 --> 04:05.980
Let's take an example that does work here.

100
04:05.980 --> 04:07.640
Let's look at 126.

101
04:07.640 --> 04:10.360
Again, we take the last digit, the last digit is six.

102
04:10.360 --> 04:13.980
We multiply that by five, so six times five gives us 30.

103
04:13.980 --> 04:16.200
And then we add 30 to the rest of the number.

104
04:16.200 --> 04:19.290
So we take 12 plus 30, we get 42.

105
04:19.290 --> 04:22.160
And we say is 42 divisible by seven?

106
04:22.160 --> 04:23.600
And in fact, it is.

107
04:23.600 --> 04:26.700
Seven goes into 42 six times exactly.

108
04:26.700 --> 04:30.409
So 126 is going to be divisible by seven.

109
04:30.409 --> 04:33.020
If we look at 128, we'll that it won't work.

110
04:33.020 --> 04:35.010
We'll take five times the last digit.

111
04:35.010 --> 04:36.810
So five times eight is 40.

112
04:36.810 --> 04:40.720
40 plus the rest of the number here, 12, gives us 52.

113
04:40.720 --> 04:42.710
Is 52 divisible by seven?

114
04:42.710 --> 04:43.620
No, it's not.

115
04:43.620 --> 04:46.840
So 128 is not going to be divisible by seven.

116
04:46.840 --> 04:49.000
The trick we use for divisibility by eight

117
04:49.000 --> 04:50.530
isn't really a trick at all.

118
04:50.530 --> 04:52.860
We're just looking to see if the last three digits

119
04:52.860 --> 04:55.380
of the number are divisible by eight,

120
04:55.380 --> 04:58.120
not individually, but together as a whole number.

121
04:58.120 --> 05:00.520
So because we're dealing with three-digit numbers here,

122
05:00.520 --> 05:02.600
we're looking at the whole number, the last three digits.

123
05:02.600 --> 05:05.380
So we say is 120 divisible by eight.

124
05:05.380 --> 05:06.870
In this case, it's not really a trick.

125
05:06.870 --> 05:08.580
But if you had a number that was much larger

126
05:08.580 --> 05:10.200
in the thousands or the millions,

127
05:10.200 --> 05:12.431
you could just take the last three digits of the number

128
05:12.431 --> 05:15.630
and say, is 120 divisible by eight.

129
05:15.630 --> 05:17.600
So instead of dividing through the whole number,

130
05:17.600 --> 05:19.230
just look at the last three digits.

131
05:19.230 --> 05:22.020
In this case, 120 is divisible by eight.

132
05:22.020 --> 05:23.889
It goes in 15 times.

133
05:23.889 --> 05:27.520
So we're going to say yes, 120 is divisible by eight.

134
05:27.520 --> 05:31.400
Which means that eight's also going to go evenly into 128,

135
05:31.400 --> 05:34.230
but it's not going to go into 126,

136
05:34.230 --> 05:36.710
because 126 divided by eight

137
05:36.710 --> 05:38.590
does not give you a whole number,

138
05:38.590 --> 05:42.040
but 128 divided by eight does give you a whole number.

139
05:42.040 --> 05:44.850
The rule for nine is very similar to the rule for three.

140
05:44.850 --> 05:46.880
You just sum up the individual digits.

141
05:46.880 --> 05:49.100
And if the sum is divisible by nine,

142
05:49.100 --> 05:51.250
then the whole number is divisible by nine.

143
05:51.250 --> 05:53.190
So we add up the digits in 120.

144
05:53.190 --> 05:56.160
We say one plus two plus zero gives us three.

145
05:56.160 --> 05:58.320
Three is not divisible by nine,

146
05:58.320 --> 06:01.330
so 120 will not be divisible by nine.

147
06:01.330 --> 06:03.520
If we add up the digits in 126,

148
06:03.520 --> 06:05.430
we get one plus two is three.

149
06:05.430 --> 06:07.120
Three plus six is nine.

150
06:07.120 --> 06:09.100
Nine is divisible by nine.

151
06:09.100 --> 06:10.740
It goes in one time, obviously.

152
06:10.740 --> 06:13.900
So 126 will also be divisible by nine.

153
06:13.900 --> 06:17.340
Here if we take 128, we say one plus two is three.

154
06:17.340 --> 06:18.910
Three plus eight is 11.

155
06:18.910 --> 06:21.100
Nine doesn't go into 11 evenly.

156
06:21.100 --> 06:25.080
So 128 will not be divisible by nine.

157
06:25.080 --> 06:28.130
And then 10 is really similar to the rule for five,

158
06:28.130 --> 06:29.660
and it should be an easy one.

159
06:29.660 --> 06:32.080
It's just if the number ends in zero,

160
06:32.080 --> 06:33.980
then the number will be divisible by 10.

161
06:33.980 --> 06:37.204
So 120, because its last digit is a zero,

162
06:37.204 --> 06:39.560
will be divisible by 10.

163
06:39.560 --> 06:42.500
But 126 ends in six, not zero.

164
06:42.500 --> 06:45.160
And 128 ends in eight, not zero.

165
06:45.160 --> 06:47.520
So those numbers will not be divisible by 10.

166
06:47.520 --> 06:49.262
So these are just some of the divisibility rules.

167
06:49.262 --> 06:50.850
There are many more.

168
06:50.850 --> 06:53.090
But hopefully these ones will help you be quicker

169
06:53.090 --> 06:55.300
when you're trying to figure out whether a large number

170
06:55.300 --> 06:56.960
like these three are divisible

171
06:56.960 --> 06:59.993
by some of these smaller numbers like two through 10.